{:nav-title "Pull"
 :content
 [:div
  [:h1 "Pull Expressions"]
  [:p "Every query so far has listed each desired attribute as a separate " [:code ":find"] " variable. "
   "Pull lets you retrieve a whole entity — or a chosen subset of its attributes — "
   "as a single map, without enumerating every binding explicitly."]
  [:p "The syntax is " [:code "(pull ?e pattern)"] " inside " [:code ":find"] ", "
   "where " [:code "pattern"] " is a vector of attribute keywords (or " [:code "[*]"] " for all of them)."]

  [:h2 "Selective pull"]
  [:p "List only the attributes you care about. "
   "Here we fetch name and speed for every Pokemon faster than 100, "
   "getting back one map per entity instead of a two-column tuple:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [:pokemon/name :stat/speed])
    :where
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]
  [:p "Each result row contains a single map. "
   "This is especially useful when returning wide entities. "
   "Without pull, every attribute needs its own variable in " [:code ":find"]
   " and its own clause in " [:code ":where"] ", and each row is a flat tuple:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find ?name ?hp ?attack ?defense ?sp-attack ?sp-defense ?speed
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name ?name]
    [?e :stat/hp ?hp]
    [?e :stat/attack ?attack]
    [?e :stat/defense ?defense]
    [?e :stat/sp-attack ?sp-attack]
    [?e :stat/sp-defense ?sp-defense]
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]
  [:p "With pull, the same Pokemon and attributes need one expression, "
   "and each row is a map that names its own values:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [:pokemon/name :stat/hp :stat/attack :stat/defense
                    :stat/sp-attack :stat/sp-defense :stat/speed])
    :where
    [?e :stat/speed ?speed]
    [(> ?speed 100)]]]

  [:h2 "Wildcard pull"]
  [:p "Use " [:code "[*]"] " to pull every attribute on the entity, "
   "including Datomic's own " [:code ":db/id"] ":"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [*])
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Charizard"]]]
  [:p "The wildcard is handy for exploration and debugging. "
   "In production code, prefer listing attributes explicitly "
   "so the shape of your data is clear and stable."]
  [:p "For a Pokemon that evolves, like Pikachu, a wildcard pull also returns " [:code ":evolution/next"] ". "
   "That attribute is a reference to another entity, so all a wildcard gives you is its " [:code ":db/id"] ". "
   "The sections below show how to follow it."]

  [:h2 "Pull with scalar find spec"]
  [:p "Combine pull with the scalar find spec (" [:code "."] ") "
   "to get a single entity map with no wrapping collection or tuple:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [:pokemon/name :pokemon/number
                    :pokemon/type :stat/hp :stat/speed]) .
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Pikachu"]]]
  [:p "The result is a bare map — easy to pass directly to the rest of your application."]

  [:h2 "Attribute options"]
  [:p "Wrap an attribute in a list to give it options. "
   [:code ":as"] " renames the key in the result, "
   [:code ":limit"] " caps how many values a cardinality-many attribute returns, "
   "and " [:code ":default"] " supplies a value when the entity doesn't have the attribute at all:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [(:pokemon/name :as "name")
                    (:pokemon/type :limit 1)
                    (:pokemon/category :default :none)])
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Bulbasaur"]]]
  [:p "Bulbasaur has two types, but " [:code ":limit 1"] " returns only one of them, and it has no "
   [:code ":pokemon/category"] ", so the default is used."]

  [:h2 "Following references"]
  [:p [:code ":evolution/next"] " is a reference: its value is another entity. "
   "To pull that entity too, put the attribute in a map with a pattern for the entity it points at:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [:pokemon/name
                    {:evolution/next [:pokemon/name :pokemon/number]}])
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Charmander"]]]
  [:p "The value of " [:code ":evolution/next"] " is now a collection of maps, one for each Pokemon "
   "Charmander evolves into, and each map follows the pattern we gave it."]

  [:h2 "Reverse lookups"]
  [:p "Put an underscore in front of the attribute name to follow a reference backwards. "
   [:code ":evolution/_next"] " finds the entities whose " [:code ":evolution/next"] " points at this one:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [:pokemon/name
                    {:evolution/_next [:pokemon/name]}])
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Charizard"]]]
  [:p "Charizard evolves from Charmeleon. Nothing had to be stored twice: "
   "a reference can be followed in either direction."]

  [:h2 "Recursion"]
  [:p "Use " [:code "..."] " instead of a pattern to follow the same reference again and again, "
   "for as long as there is something to follow. "
   "A number, like " [:code "{:evolution/next 2}"] ", limits how many levels to follow:"]
  [:ui/runnable "pokemon"
   [:find (pull ?e [:pokemon/name
                    {:evolution/next ...}])
    :where
    [?e :pokemon/name "Charmander"]]]
  [:p "This gives Charmander's whole evolution line as a nested map."]
  [:ui/try-tip
   [:p "Change " [:code "\"Charmander\""] " to " [:code "\"Eevee\""] " in the recursive pull to see it branch three ways. "
    "Or add " [:code ":stat/attack"] " and " [:code ":stat/defense"] " to the selective pull pattern at the top of this page."]]]}
